NAME
       ar - pack and unpack archives

SYNOPSIS
       ar c <archive> <file>...
       ar x <archive> [directory]
       ar t <archive>

DESCRIPTION
       Reads and writes the archive formats other systems use, so a
       file packed here opens elsewhere and the other way round.

       The format follows the archive's name:

         .zip              a zip archive
         .tar              a tar archive, uncompressed
         .tar.gz, .tgz     a tar archive, gzip-compressed
         .gz               one file, gzip-compressed

       On extracting, the name is not trusted: the contents are looked
       at, so an archive named wrongly still opens.

COMMANDS
       c      Create. A directory given here is packed with everything
              inside it, however deep.

       x      Extract. With a directory after the archive, everything
              lands under it; without one, into the current directory.
              Directories inside the archive are created as needed.

       t      List what is inside, with sizes, without writing
              anything.

COMPRESSION
       Each file is compressed on its own in a zip, and the whole
       archive at once in a .tar.gz. A file that does not get smaller
       is stored as it is rather than made bigger.

       Reading handles both stored and deflated entries. Zip archives
       using other methods are named and skipped rather than silently
       mangled.

EXAMPLE
         ar c project.zip src doc readme.txt
         ar t project.zip
         ar x project.zip /tmp/unpacked

SEE ALSO
       tar(1), inflate(3)
